 Gooch Shading is a non-photorealistic illumination model for volume rendering that maps surface orientation relative to a light source to a warm-to-cool color gradient to convey shape. It is distinguished from standard Gooch shading by its specific integration with on-the-fly central difference normal estimation in rasterization pipelines, ensuring consistent visual output across different extraction methods (rasterization vs. ray-marching).
 
-### Cool to Warm Shading
-A tone-based shading model that blends between cool colors in regions facing away from the light and warm colors in regions facing the light source, often used with silhouette edges [S5, unofficial]. Unlike physically based shading (e.g., Phong), which may lose shape information in regions facing away from the light, cool-to-warm shading enhances perception of shape [S5, unofficial].
+### [Cool to Warm Shading](13943-cool-to-warm-shading.md)
+Cool to warm shading is a non-photorealistic illumination model that enhances the perception of 3D shape by mapping surface orientation relative to the viewer or light source to a color gradient, assigning warmer colors to facing regions and cooler colors to receding regions. Unlike physically based models like Phong, which may obscure shape information in shadowed areas, this method ensures that orientation cues are preserved across the entire visible surface. It builds on [Volume Illustration Tone Shading](805-volume-illustration-tone-shading.md) by replacing the signed dot product illumination term with a direct view/light-dependent color mapping.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time and memory complexity:** Not given in the sources. [S3, unofficial]
-* **Measured performance:** Not given in the sources. [S3, unofficial]
-* **Strengths:** Enhances shape perception by providing warm/cool cues based on orientation. Combines well with gaseous illumination and transfer functions [S3, unofficial]. The modification to use only positive dot products for directed illumination provides a more familiar diffuse lighting appearance [S2][S3, unofficial].
-* **Limitations:** Can become confusing with too many light sources [S3, unofficial]. Relies on accurate gradient estimation.
-* **When to use:** When the goal is to enhance the perception of 3D shape and internal structures in volume data using a painterly, illustrative style [S2].
-* **When not to use:** When photorealism is required, or when the scene complexity with multiple lights would lead to visual confusion [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of volume samples per ray and the number of light sources $N_L$. Each sample requires gradient computation, dot products, and color interpolation [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume data, gradient field (if precomputed), and transfer function [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method is computationally efficient as it avoids complex light transport calculations, relying on simple dot products and color interpolation [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enhances shape perception and depth cues effectively; preserves basic tissue colors while adding illustrative shading; works well with medical and scientific data [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can produce confusing results with too many lights; relies on accurate gradient estimation; the illustrative style may not be suitable for all scientific visualization tasks requiring photorealism [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When enhancing 3D shape perception and depth relationships in volume data is prioritized over photorealism, particularly in medical or scientific visualization [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When photorealistic rendering is required or when the scene contains complex lighting interactions that cannot be approximated by simple directional lights [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Volume Illustration Tone Shading is based on the Gooch tone shading model proposed by Gooch et al. [Gooch98] [S2]. It was adapted for volume rendering by implementing an illumination model similar to Gooch tone shading but modified for volume samples, specifically by using only the positive dot product for the illuminated object contribution [S2][S3, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader "Volume Illustration" framework, which combines direct volume rendering with non-photorealistic rendering techniques to produce a wide range of illustration styles [S2]. Examples of its application include medical volumes (liver, kidney, tomato MRI) and scientific data (iron protein) [S2].
+Volume Illustration Tone Shading is based on the Gooch tone shading model proposed by Gooch et al. in 1998 [S3, S4, unofficial]. It was adapted for volume rendering by Wilson, Lum, and Ma in their work on "Volume Illustration: Non-Photorealistic Rendering of Volume Models" (2000/2001) [S2][S3, S4, unofficial]. The method was applied to medical volumes (liver/kidney CT) and scientific data (iron protein, tomato MRI) to demonstrate its effectiveness in revealing internal structures and enhancing 3D shape perception [S3, unofficial].
 
